A||||2||Phaeton||Windshield broken and almost off||||9||
Z||000000||Phaeton||06-07-2000||01:22 AM||||The roads up here are built on permafrost, lots of heaves on any surface more than two years old.<BR>I just ignore them and drive regular (100+) whenever conditions allow.  The Fiero spends time airborn fairly often.<P>The windshield seal came loose, from the passenger wiper pivot all the way around the side and across the top.  Also along the driver corner down to six inches from the dash.<BR>Its broken (lots) in a line from the base where the glue held up towards the driver's chin level.<P>When the windshield broke loose it took large gouges out of the top of both sides, right into the fiberglass??<BR>Isn't this supposed to be plastic?<P>Am I abusing the Fiero past it's limits? or was the windshield installed with out of date glue?  I have never had this happen to a vehicle before now, but this is the only car I have felt comfortable with while airborn.<P>Is it because it's a t-top?  When I started taking things apart, it sure did look like the original roof frame was cut with a Sawzall.  It can't be as rigid.<P>Should I buy an 85 em4 thats for sale and start swapping in parts?  The 85 has the whole roof still intact.<P>Mostly I'm hoping the Fiero is so tough this is not my fault. I was just coming up on 90 when this happened at the end of a good sized (150'?) sinkhole.  Wide, not deep.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||06-07-2000||10:40 AM||||<BR>Just curious, but is there are rust under the windshield? If so, that may have been the cause of the weakened structure.  Rust has a great way of destroying structural rigidity.<P>If the 85 is in good shape and cheap, it may be worth purchasing. It will end up having more parts than you can may realize, except the suspension, if you are driving an 88.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 06-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-07-2000||10:55 AM||||Several of the Fiero body panels are fiberglass reinforced. Fiero windshields are bonded with special adhesives to the body, so if there was enough flexing (obviously there was) something has to give. I've seen abused Fieros with cracks just above the quarter windows too. My advice is to slow down for the big bumps. Fieros are tough, but not indestructable, especially if abused. T-tops will allow more flexing too, especially if not braced properly. Hope you get it fixed back up, good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-07-2000).]||||reg||1
